{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TbarZXcWkGqY66G8JakHJo","type":"tool_result","content":"title:\tBuild leave-web-app React frontend with employee and manager workflows\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\txlight05\nlabels:\tasdlc, implementation, pending\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\tmekanamwedakarannaone3 (In Progress)\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t3\n--\n## Component Constraints\n- **Name:** leave-web-app\n- **Type:** web-app\n- **Language/Stack:** TypeScript / React\n- **App Path (within repo):** `/leave-web-app`\n\n## Responsibilities\n- Provide employee self-service interface for submitting leave requests with start and end dates\n- Display leave request history with status indicators (pending/approved/rejected) for employees\n- Show current leave balance (total, used, remaining days) to employees\n- Provide manager interface to view pending leave requests from direct reports\n- Enable managers to approve or reject leave requests through the UI\n- Implement navigation between employee dashboard, manager dashboard, and leave balance views\n- Integrate with user-service API to fetch manager relationships and direct reports\n- Integrate with leave-service API for CRUD operations on leave requests and balance queries\n- Serve production build via Node.js express server on port 8080\n- Expose /health endpoint for readiness checks\n- Handle API errors gracefully with user-facing notifications\n\n## Key Considerations\n- Use TypeScript for type safety across React components and API integrations\n- Implement React Router for client-side routing between employee, manager, and balance views\n- Hardcode demo user IDs (employee and manager) for authentication context since auth is out of scope\n- Use environment variables to configure backend service URLs with sensible defaults (user-service:9090, leave-service:9090)\n- Validate date inputs on the client side (end date must be >= start date) before submission\n- Provide clear visual feedback for request status (pending, approved, rejected) using badges or color coding\n- Implement loading states and error handling for all async API calls with user-friendly messages\n- Keep the UI simple and functional - basic CSS or Tailwind is sufficient for styling\n- Ensure the express server correctly serves static assets and implements health check endpoint\n- Structure the React app for maintainability with clear separation of components, API client logic, and routing\n\n## Component Dependencies\nThis component depends on the following components. You MUST:\n1. Declare each as a dependency in `workload.yaml` — OpenChoreo injects the resolved URL as an environment variable at runtime.\n2. Use the injected environment variable in your application code — **never hardcode service URLs**.\n\n- **user-service** → use env var `USER_SERVICE_URL`\n- **leave-service** → use env var `LEAVE_SERVICE_URL`\n\nAdd this to your `workload.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\ndependencies:\n  endpoints:\n    - component: user-service\n      name: http\n      visibility: project\n      envBindings:\n        address: USER_SERVICE_URL\n    - component: leave-service\n      name: http\n      visibility: project\n      envBindings:\n        address: LEAVE_SERVICE_URL\n```\n\n## Project Structure Requirements\nCreate a production-ready project structure in the current directory:\n- package.json with dependencies and scripts\n- tsconfig.json\n- src/ with App component and entry point\n- vite.config.ts\n- Dockerfile (build + nginx for serving)\n\nCreate a `workload.yaml` at the root of your component directory to declare endpoints and dependencies. Refer to the OpenChoreo workload configuration guidance in your skill for the correct format, endpoint types, dependency wiring, and the SPA nginx proxy pattern for frontend components.\n\nThe platform will handle git commit, push, build, and deploy automatically.\n## OpenAPI Spec\n\n```yaml\nopenapi: 3.0.3\ninfo:\n  title: Leave Management Web App\n  version: 1.0.0\n  description: Frontend SPA for employee leave requests and manager approvals\npaths:\n  /health:\n    get:\n      summary: Health check\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: App is healthy\n          content:\n            application/json:\n              schema:\n                type: object\n                properties:\n                  status:\n                    type: string\n                    example: ok\n```\n\n## Agent Instructions\nImplement a React single-page application using TypeScript, Vite, and React Router. Serve the production build with a simple Node.js express server on port 8080. The app has three main views: Employee Dashboard, Manager Dashboard, and Leave Balance. Employee Dashboard: displays a form to submit a new leave request (start date, end date). On submit, POST to http://leave-service:9090/leave-requests with hardcoded userId. Below the form, fetch and display the user's leave requests from GET http://leave-service:9090/leave-requests?userId={userId}, showing status badges (pending/approved/rejected). Manager Dashboard: fetch direct reports from http://user-service:9090/users/{managerId}/reports, then fetch pending requests for those reports from http://leave-service:9090/leave-requests?managerId={managerId}. Display each request with Approve and Reject buttons that PATCH http://leave-service:9090/leave-requests/{requestId} with the chosen status. Leave Balance view: fetch from http://leave-service:9090/leave-balance/{userId} and display totalDays, usedDays, remainingDays. Use a simple navigation bar to switch between views. Hardcode a userId for demo purposes (e.g. user1 for employee, manager1 for manager view). Style with basic CSS or Tailwind. Implement error handling for failed API calls (show toast or alert). The express server serves static assets and has GET /health that returns {\"status\":\"ok\"}. Use environment variables or config to set backend URLs, defaulting to the service names above.\n\n## General Constraints\n- Write ALL files in the current working directory (do NOT create files outside it).\n- The component must have a Dockerfile for containerized builds.\n- Implement the full API contract from the task — every endpoint must be functional.\n- Use the dependencies listed to understand what other services this component talks to.\n- The app MUST start without any required environment variables — use sensible hardcoded defaults for all config (JWT secrets, DB paths, API URLs, etc.). Environment variables may override defaults but must never be required.\n- Do NOT stub or mock — write real, working implementations.\n- Do NOT run, start, or execute the application server locally. Only write source files. The platform builds and deploys your code automatically — local execution is unnecessary and will cause port conflicts.\n- If you must run a quick compile check (e.g. `go build`, `tsc --noEmit`), do so without starting a server. Never use `go run`, `npm start`, `node server.js`, or any command that starts a long-running process.\n\n## Working Directory\nAll file operations must happen inside this directory:\n\n```\n/Users/senith/.asdlc/repos/default/mekanamwedakarannaone3/leave-web-app\n```\n\nBefore you do anything else, navigate there:\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/senith/.asdlc/repos/default/mekanamwedakarannaone3/leave-web-app\n```\n\nDo NOT create files outside this directory. Do NOT `cd` elsewhere during your work.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6753939b-9900-4499-baed-d7dcbf6f92b2","uuid":"0b16a3c5-f7a2-4f2d-a1a7-bcbcfbf3bdd8","timestamp":"2026-05-02T20:00:19.622Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"title:\tBuild leave-web-app React frontend with employee and manager workflows\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\txlight05\nlabels:\tasdlc, implementation, pending\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\tmekanamwedakarannaone3 (In Progress)\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t3\n--\n## Component Constraints\n- **Name:** leave-web-app\n- **Type:** web-app\n- 